The process of voting in France traditionally was not high-tech. Voters were locked in the booth in order to get a pre-printed sheet of paper, which indicated the name of the corresponding candidate, and put it in an envelope.
After identifying the voter, the latter drops an envelope into an urn for voting and puts his signature in the list of voters.
All this procedure is carried out in strict accordance with France's suffrage.
Beginning in 1988, the ballot boxes must be transparent. This is done so that voters and observers have the opportunity to confirm that at the time of the start of voting there were no envelopes and that there were no other envelopes (except for those who were pulfilled and counted by voters in the ballot boxes).
In the evening, according to certain procedures, volunteers who are under strict urgency count the votes.
